<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Prinitza</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1263">1263</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Prinitza was fought in 1263 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympia</a> in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> between a small Frankish force of the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> and a much larger Byzantine army. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> won a surprising victory, halting the Byzantine advance from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mystras">Mistras</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pelagonia">Battle of Pelagonia</a> in 1259 the forces of the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII Palaiologos</a> killed or captured most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> nobles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>, among them Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-villehardouin">William II of Villehardouin</a>. To win his freedom William became Michael's vassal and agreed to hand over fortresses in the south-east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a>; on his release in early 1262 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monemvasia">Monemvasia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mystras">Mystras</a> and the district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a> passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>. The settlement could not last. In July Pope Urban IV nullified William's oaths to the emperor, and William sought joint action with the Venetians and the Pope.</p><p>Late in 1262 William toured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a> with an armed retinue, and the alarmed Byzantine governor Michael Kantakouzenos asked the emperor for help. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII</a> sent the parakoimomenos John Makrenos with 1,500 Turkish mercenaries and about 2,000 Anatolian Greeks, carried on Genoese ships, and then his half-brother, the sebastokrator Constantine Palaiologos, with 1,000 more men. Constantine built forts against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> of Mount Taygetos and besieged Lacedaemon, while many of William's Greek subjects went over to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> and the other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> princes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> refused him aid.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Abandoning the siege, Constantine marched up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurotas">Eurotas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alpheios">Alfeios</a> towards the Achaean capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andravida">Andravida</a>, held in William's absence by John of Katavas, a brave man but old and suffering from gout. The only detailed account is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-the-morea">Chronicle of the Morea</a>, whose accuracy has been questioned. It has Katavas march out with 300 or 312 men against a Byzantine army of fifteen to twenty thousand; these figures are certainly inflated, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> still greatly outnumbered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> force. At a narrow defile at Prinitza, where the overconfident Byzantines were reportedly dancing and singing, Katavas attacked and routed them. Constantine barely escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mystras">Mystras</a>, and Katavas returned to Andravida without pursuing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Constantine regrouped and campaigned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> again the next year, but his Turkish mercenaries, complaining of lack of pay, defected to the Achaeans, and William defeated the weakened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-makryplagi">Battle of Makryplagi</a>. The two battles ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII</a>'s efforts to recover the whole of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a> and secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> rule there for more than a generation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Prinitza” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Franks won notable victories at <b>Prinitza</b> in 1263 and Makryplagi in 1264, but the frontier moved steadily westward.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Military</a></li></ul></section>
